    "engineJob": 4210,
    "sha256": "350b5b9e103c186fc9f9827b9fe404bd0eeaf87bbdc3e9a12df42c69f514597a",
    "markdown": "# Game Studio\n\nPeople searching game studio Philippines are usually looking for a team that can handle art, engineering, testing or shipping a title, not for an SEO lecture. If that is your studio, the site still has to answer the brief: who you are, what you ship, and which URL owns the game. A finished build with the wrong page in search is how partners and players miss you.\n\nWe work this from Quezon City as Shinobi, a studio of Bright Forge SEO, the parent company. The Philippines is a real production base for games, art, QA and live ops. The search problem is not “more blogs”. It is which URL owns which question, whether Google can read it, and whether the visitor can act.\n\nIf you are deciding what to fix first, start with the pages that already carry the title. Then decide whether the studio itself needs a commercial page. Do not invent a content mill to cover for a launch URL that never named the game in text.\n\n[Google’s SEO Starter Guide](https://developers.google.com/search/docs/fundamentals/seo-starter-guide) still applies: crawlable pages, clear titles, useful text, and links that explain how the site fits together. A WebGL hero does not cancel that.\n\n## Give The Title A Home\n\nEvery shipped game needs one URL that can answer “what is this, where do I get it, and is it still supported”. That is not the same as a news post from reveal week. Reveal posts go stale. Store widgets break. Support moves to Discord and never comes back to the site.\n\nWrite the title page as if a player found it from search, not from your trailer. Name the game in the heading. Say the platforms. Say whether it is out, in early access, or no longer sold. Link the live store or waitlist. Keep patch notes on a connected URL so the title page does not become a changelog dump.\n\nIf you have a franchise, give the franchise a hub and each title its own page. One blob that lists five games will rank for none of them cleanly.\n\n## Separate Studio Search From Player Search\n\nStudio search is B2B. It is “game art studio Philippines”, co-development, Unity work, Unreal work, live-ops support. Player search is the title, genre, platform and troubleshooting. Mixing both on the homepage makes the studio look vague and the game look unfinished.\n\nThe studio page should say what you actually sell: art, engineering, publishing support, a specific genre, a specific platform. Name the locations you truly work from. Quezon City is ours. Do not paste Cebu or Davao as offices you do not have. If you take remote work for teams in those cities, say that as service area, not as a branch.\n\nPlayer pages should not pretend to be an agency brochure. They should help someone play or buy.\n\n## Make The Page Readable Before The Motion\n\nSearch does not watch your intro animation. If the project name, contribution and store link only appear after JavaScript, check the first HTML and the rendered DOM against a sentence you care about. A portfolio can look complete in a browser and still leave Google with a shell.\n\nKeep important facts in text. Trailers can sit beside that text. Do not replace the explanation with a video that never loads on a slow connection.\n\nThis is the same rendering check we use on creative portfolios. Games make it worse because title sites often hide body copy behind canvas layers.\n\n## Map Launches Without Cloning URLs\n\nA launch needs a date, a store, a press kit, and a place for post-launch support. It does not need a new URL for every slogan. If two phrases ask the same question, they belong on the same page.\n\nWhen a title moves from coming soon to released, update the existing URL. Redirect campaign microsites that will die. Keep the URL players already shared.\n\nInternal links should point to the page that owns the next question: from trailer to title, from title to support, from studio to the work you want more of.\n\n## What We Do Not Do\n\nWe do not sell ranking promises for competitive game names. We do not stuff “Philippines” into every heading. We do not recommend fifty thin city pages for a studio that ships worldwide from one office.\n\nWe do diagnose crawl blockers, title ownership, store and support paths, and the studio’s own commercial pages. That work lives on our [games and interactive SEO](https://shinobiseo.com/industries/gaming) page. If you want it applied to a live title, [request a review](https://shinobiseo.com/contact).\n\nA useful first pass is small: one title URL, one studio URL, Search Console coverage, and a list of queries you already see. Then decide whether you need more pages at all.\n\n## Proof And Measurement\n\nParent-company case work from Bright Forge can inform method. It does not let us invent player counts for your title. Measure indexation, the queries the title page actually earns, store clicks, and support contacts. If impressions rise and downloads do not, the page may be answering the wrong job.\n\nKeep analytics on the title domain you control. A store listing is not a substitute for a page you can still edit after the campaign team leaves.\n\nGame search is messy because the product keeps changing. The site has to change with it, on purpose, not by publishing another recap.\n## After The Patch\n\nA title site that only worked on launch day is a liability. Patches, DLC, season passes and server shutdowns all change what a player needs to know. The title URL should absorb those facts without becoming a dump of patch notes. Put notes on a connected page and keep the title page responsible for identity, availability and the next place to play.\n\nCommunities move to Discord, Reddit and wikis. That is fine. Those places should not be the only explanation of what the game is. A wiki you do not control can outrank you for your own name if your page is a trailer with no text.\n\nPublishers with several studios need a clear split between publisher, studio and title. Players do not care about your org chart. They care which page answers the question they typed.\n\nIf localisation exists, each language needs a crawlable URL and a proper hreflang plan, not a widget that rewrites the same address. Shipping English only is honest. Pretending you localised in the template is not.\n\nStore widgets fail. Keep a plain-language fallback for platform and region. If a store is regional, say so. Do not send a Philippine player to a storefront that will not take them.\n\nWhen a game is delisted, say so on the same URL. Nostalgia traffic still arrives. A silent 404 teaches Google to prefer a reseller.\n\n## Studio Sites Versus Title Sites\n\nA studio with three live titles often tries to rank all of them from shinobi-style homepage copy. That fails. The studio site should win studio queries. Each title should live on a hostname or directory you are willing to keep for years.\n\nSubdomains, store pages and fan wikis will compete. You cannot delete them. You can be the clearest official source. Official does not mean stuffing the registered name into every H2. It means consistent names, dates and links.\n\nIf marketing wants a new brand world for every season, give it a campaign URL with a redirect date. Do not let seasonal art direction reset the canonical title path.\n\nQA builds, staging and press-only hosts must stay out of the index. A single leftover noindex mistake on production is worse than a noisy staging site, because it hides the real game.\n\nMeta title: Game Studio | Shinobi\nMeta description: Title pages, studio pages and launch URLs for a game studio in the Philippines, without turning the site into an SEO lecture.\n"
